  "gotchas": [
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "RESIDENCY IS THE POINT OF THIS RUN. The n-gram table is 26.82 GiB of the checkpoint, held on NVMe and gathered about 16 rows at a time out of 320,001,536, so a short workload almost never touches a row twice and the table does not warm on its own. Residency was SET to a target before this run, not observed: 0.06% measured with mincore(2) immediately before the workload. Compare this run only against the other runs of this same config_id in this repository, which are the other points of the series; a run of this configuration that does not state its residency is not comparable to any of them."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "--spec-type ngram-mod is ON, which is the case where residency was expected to matter most: the drafter proposes spans of 50-60 tokens from repetition in the context, and verifying a span touches many n-gram rows at once rather than one per token. Speculation is exact, so output is byte-identical to running without it, but throughput varies up to 3x by how much of the output already appears in the prompt. Never compare a decode figure here against a cell run with a different --spec-type."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "--parallel 1 is forced, not chosen. This build gives llama-server ONE slot, and the recipe reports that raising it aborts with a GGML_ASSERT in qwen4exp.cpp:284. Concurrent requests queue rather than crash."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "Workloads warm the table as they run, so residency at the end of a run is higher than at the start. The figure quoted here is the state the workload STARTED from, which is the variable being controlled; the end state is recorded in the pull request. This self-warming is the most likely reason a short benchmark understates any cold penalty."
    }
  ],
