    "notes": "Box WAS dedicated: this DGX Spark runs no desktop session, and no compile or dev server ran on it during the sweep. Isolation check: the only route to it from outside the box is an SSH tunnel opened by a reverse proxy on the same private network, and that tunnel was stopped before the first run and stayed stopped throughout, so no external client could contend for the GPU. The GPU compute-app list sampled immediately before this workload contained exactly: /home/bakeer/llamacpp-qwen4exp/build/bin/llama-server. NVIDIA DGX Spark (ASUS Ascent GX10, GB10 / SM121, 128 GB unified memory, ~121 GiB usable) on Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, kernel 6.17.0-1029-nvidia aarch64, driver 580.173.02, CUDA 13.0. Swap is off. Engine is llama.cpp built from the unmerged PR #27742 (qwen4exp architecture support) at commit 035e227, with the canreuse-qwen4exp patch from github.com/0xBakeer/qwen38-flash-next-spark applied so CUDA graph capture engages. Model is unsloth/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF UD-Q4_K_XL, four shards, 111,323,630,080 bytes. Server flags: -lm mmap -ot per_layer_token_embd=CPU --n-gpu-layers 999 --ctx-size 262144 --parallel 1 --spec-type ngram-mod --temp 1.0 --top-p 0.95 --top-k 20. THIS RUN IS ONE POINT OF A DELIBERATE RESIDENCY SERIES. The 51.2B-parameter n-gram/PLE table is one tensor of 26.82 GiB served from NVMe through the OS page cache, and its residency is an independent variable that no flag records. It was set before this run rather than left to chance: posix_fadvise(DONTNEED) over the tensor's byte range, then a read of a prefix sized to the target fraction, both verified with mincore(2). The gather addresses about 320 million rows by a 3-gram hash, so what a lookup sees is the resident FRACTION and not which part is resident, which is why a contiguous prefix is a valid way to sit at a chosen level. Measured residency for this run: 0.06% immediately before the workload started. The series exists because the recipe reported cold-versus-warm at up to +42% with ngram-mod on, a later re-measurement at 17.7% versus 58.1% found no difference, and nobody had tested the ~2% a real startup actually lands at. llama-server binds 127.0.0.1 only and the harness connects to it directly."
